{"agent_id":"pp3g-87Fl-review-the-workflow-topology-proposal-durability-a80a","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T22:35:05.216927813Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"dc1e02ef-9bd8-47b3-a73f-cae00a3bbcce","comment_uuid":"54437371-f584-4f7d-9ef5-605b0b272515","author":"pp3g-87Fl-review-the-workflow-topology-proposal-durability-a80a","content":"[action] Completed independent durability/systems analysis: topology sound at producer-consumer asymmetry, but proposal is conditional pending explicit lifecycle, ownership, escalation, and load controls.","created_at":"2026-08-07T22:35:05.209632784Z","kind":"note","signed_by":"SHA256:NzxQtOqrvipE74gaO7hxajW/ogMMSdLY62Oegpdc7C8","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgtZRMXBo20CbyTutR+R+YbAs8t412pXCkny2H4U5eILYAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAx79nRQRFZd//WgqOsqQ0TNaOrKx8UILZYGs9VgnzcWft9XdiD6RetyCZNP39JphNTu79kmamTUoyppX1WyMXAQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:NzxQtOqrvipE74gaO7hxajW/ogMMSdLY62Oegpdc7C8","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgtZRMXBo20CbyTutR+R+YbAs8t412pXCkny2H4U5eILYAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQK0fstC7rYmtwb4upX42PYuaxoL3LJs9RWFk1RRaf8CGY1jIQFrpBpWzgfz6vzw+rmbC/Imnn2iZlI/fK7lE4wg="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-87Fl-review-the-workflow-topology-proposal-durability-a80a","agent_seq":6,"timestamp":"2026-08-07T22:35:20.090337042Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"dc1e02ef-9bd8-47b3-a73f-cae00a3bbcce","comment_uuid":"b81d83ae-681a-4443-a803-4945d373aa51","author":"pp3g-87Fl-review-the-workflow-topology-proposal-durability-a80a","content":"## Verdict: CONDITIONAL\n\nThe topology finding is directionally sound: a producer that can cheaply verify a claim while a thin consumer cannot is a genuine durability boundary, and decision-gating claims crossing it deserve explicit provenance and verification. However, calling it the *dominant* unguarded failure point is not yet established by the evidence presented; the same asymmetry exists at operator→orchestrator approval, workflow→workflow handoffs, and escalation/recovery boundaries. The topology should be stated as a high-risk class, not an exclusive failure diagnosis.\n\n### Findings\n\n1. **STOP B is correctly located, but its contract is underspecified (MUST FIX before adoption).** A pre-consumption audit is the right control point because it is the last place to reject a producer result before the thin consumer acts. It must be a durable, machine-checkable handoff: immutable wave/run and artifact identity, schema/version validation, provenance and freshness, explicit tested/not-tested fields, and an atomic snapshot or equivalent TOCTOU protection. A prose audit can pass and then leave the consumer reading a changed, superseded, or differently scoped result. Define who owns rejection and the bounded escalation path; otherwise STOP B becomes an unbounded bounce loop or a serial orchestrator bottleneck.\n\n2. **STOP A is useful but should not gate every plan.** Post-plan testability review is a cheap filter for decision-bearing assumptions, not a guarantee that the proposed test is discriminating or that the producer has authority to make the claim. Require it only for assumptions that can change routing, approval, mutation, or recovery; record “not cheaply testable” with an explicit escalation rather than forcing a cosmetic test.\n\n3. **STOP C has the right risk-sensitive intent, but “high stakes” needs a policy and a fallback.** Install/merge are examples, not a complete class. Include irreversible external effects, permissions, migrations, recovery actions, and claims with stale/ambiguous provenance. Set a bounded retry/escalation budget and an operator decision state. Otherwise a failed L2 check can either silently downgrade to L1 or generate infinite re-review. #136 demonstrates that timeout/nudge/relaunch paths need run identity, leases, cooldowns, independent liveness, and a recovery ledger; #150 demonstrates that a timeout/repair path can broaden an unbounded retry loop and duplicate non-idempotent work.\n\n4. **Important unguarded edges remain.** The proposal needs explicit controls for: operator→orchestrator intent/approval and handoff of authority; cross-workflow claims whose producer context, artifact version, or policy scope differs; stale Crosslink/hub reads and concurrent issue/worktree ownership; reviewer/provider hangs and partial or lost verdicts; escalation ownership when a stop cannot run; cancellation and resume after crash; and idempotency when a consumer retries an action. STOP B only covers one edge and cannot make these durable automatically.\n\n5. **Model-agreement collection is a good non-gating design, but not yet a complete lifecycle.** #241 has the right foundations: fixed claim classes, normalized PASS/CONDITIONAL/FAIL vocabulary, finding-level agreement (not merely verdict-level), live versus reconstructed confidence, divergence notes, and missing-verdict handling without blocking the workflow. Add an immutable wave ID and schema version; exact links/hashes to each source verdict and reviewed artifact; model/provider/version/prompt or rubric identity; explicit partial/failed reviewer states; append-only correction/adjudication records; idempotent closeout writes; and a distinction between reconstructed confidence and confidence in the underlying claim. Back-fill should preserve uncertainty per row/finding, not only one wave-level label. For drift, version taxonomies/rubrics, track reviewer mix and selection bias, and periodically compare agreement, 1-of-N catches, disagreement, missingness, and false-consensus rates over time. Otherwise historical and live rows are not safely comparable and routing recommendations can fossilize.\n\n6. **Thin-orchestrator preservation is plausible, not demonstrated.** “Zero-cost byproduct” is true only if the orchestrator already has structured verdicts and the closeout write is asynchronous/non-gating. STOP B audits, reconciliation of partial results, escalation, and agreement synthesis can turn the orchestrator into a substantive reviewer under concurrency. Preserve thinness with bounded schemas/signals, queue-based or batched closeout, per-wave deadlines, idempotent writes, backpressure/concurrency limits, and a circuit breaker/manual escalation mode. Never make the orchestrator re-review full evidence inline; delegate L2 and retain a durable pending state.\n\n### Required preconditions for PASS\n\n- Specify the STOP B handoff schema, atomicity/freshness/provenance checks, ownership, and bounded rejection/escalation state machine.\n- Define the risk taxonomy and deterministic trigger/timeout rules for STOP A and STOP C, including non-testable assumptions and external side effects.\n- Add lifecycle/versioning/provenance/drift controls to the model-agreement collection and make back-fill confidence granular.\n- Demonstrate load behavior with concurrent waves, missing/hung reviewers, stale reads, crash/resume, repeated closeout, and bounded STOP B/C failures; verify the orchestrator remains coordination-only.\n\nThis is a **CONDITIONAL**, not a rejection of the core topology. The proposal identifies a real and important boundary, but the controls currently describe review locations more than durable state-machine contracts.","created_at":"2026-08-07T22:35:20.084011325Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:NzxQtOqrvipE74gaO7hxajW/ogMMSdLY62Oegpdc7C8","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgtZRMXBo20CbyTutR+R+YbAs8t412pXCkny2H4U5eILYAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAvayR0W7af94S/nzbU+b1jX+D4bDURLBYf99xM2m6MsflcCatFV5Cwf8rItBCmfAq3Bj2hmlVmOUe+xp5iFCPCA=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:NzxQtOqrvipE74gaO7hxajW/ogMMSdLY62Oegpdc7C8","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgtZRMXBo20CbyTutR+R+YbAs8t412pXCkny2H4U5eILYAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQLMYHjK494/XQlRIWtl2bS5yJycv8qR2afXkw4EMz0WGELvNajsmpmOMIzS5ZBsva6VjjUB4x2uDdgzREgBqYgA="}
